A Java service sorts account balances (as `int`s, in pence) with this comparator. Balances range from about −2 billion to +2 billion. What goes wrong?

```java
// Subtraction can overflow an int.
list.sort((a, b) -> a - b);
```

This Java statement is an intentionally faulty comparator example, with list supplied by the caller.

What does this JavaScript print?

```js
// Default sorting compares strings.
console.log([10, 9, 1].sort());
```

What does this Java code print?

```java
// Missing keys encode insertion points.
int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40};
System.out.println(
  Arrays.binarySearch(a, 25));
```

questions.workedExample:
Track the kept set (root = max):

```
5: push        {5}
1: push        {1, 5}  root 5
9: 9 < 5? no   ignore
3: 3 < 5? yes  replace -> {1, 3}
7: 7 < 3? no   ignore
2: 2 < 3? yes  replace -> {1, 2}
```

Result `[1, 2]`, with 2 calls to `heapreplace`. Each replacement costs O(log k); each rejection costs one comparison.

Insert into the lower half when the new value is no larger than that half’s maximum; otherwise insert into the upper half. Rebalance sizes afterward.

```
5:  lo{5}        hi{}
15: lo{5}        hi{15}
1:  lo{1,5}      hi{15}
3:  lo{1,3,5} -> too big, move 5
    lo{1,3}      hi{5,15}
8:  8 > 3: hi{5,8,15} -> move 5
    lo{1,3,5}    hi{8,15}
```

The lower-heap root is five, so the median is five. With n values inserted so far, each insertion is O(log n) and reading the median is O(1).
